These tables contain
1. The values of the auxiliary parameters a, b and n obtained by fitting the approximations to the numerical spectra (Na, Nb, Nn) and cumulative probabilities (Pa, Pb, Pn)
2. Certain values of the numerical functions that are calculated beforehand


1. The parameters
- "SynchInt1MPade(4;0,2,3)INFPFirst030226.csv" contains the values of the auxiliary parameters when all of them are kept free with different values of chi_electron
- "SynchInt1MPade(4;0,2,3)INFPFirstAandB030226.csv" contains the values of the approximating form of the auxiliary parameter n, and the values for the parameters
a and b that are obtained after the approximations are refitted using the approximating form of n
- "BWHO{4;0,1,2}PadeparamALL100526.csv" and "BWHO{4;0,1,2}PadeparametersOnlyAandB100526.csv" are equivalent dataframes but for the Breit-Wheeler approximations


2. The precalculated values of the numerical functions
- The "T_Synch_tab030226.csv" and "T_BW_tab090226.csv" dataframes contain the values of the auxiliary functions with different values of chi
- The "N_Synch_tab030226.csv" and "N_BW_tab090226.csv" dataframes contain the integrals of the spectra with different values of r, such that the columns from left to right
correspond to the chi-values in the dataframes "T_Synch_tab030226.csv" and "T_BW_tab090226.csv". These are used to obtain the values of the cumulative probabilities instead of
doing the integration repeatedly.


These dataframes are already used in the files that contain the code for figure creation. Only the auxiliary parameter dataframes are required to be used to create
figures equivalent to those in the paper (unless the user wants to find the optimalvalues by themselves) and the rest are used only to speed up the figure creation.

